/**
 * Scoped DB Wrapper
 *
 * Wraps a Drizzle db instance so every statement carries the target table's
 * FIREWALL — the same generated `buildFirewallConditions` CRUD applies
 * (see ./firewall-registry). Access markers (PUBLIC, AUTHENTICATED, …) and an
 * action's own authority decision never widen it: a caller whose claim the
 * table's firewall requires, and who doesn't carry it, sees nothing.
 *
 * Tables with no resource module (auth tables, bare Drizzle tables) fall back
 * to the auth-provider column rules (organizationId / ownerId / teamId /
 * deletedAt), and deny when a claim those rules need is absent.
 *
 * Usage:
 *   const db = createScopedDb(unsafeDb, ctx);
 *   await db.select().from(claims).where(eq(claims.status, 'active'));
 *   // → WHERE status = 'active' AND <claims' firewall>
 *
 * Drizzle's relational query API (db.query.<table>.findFirst/findMany) is
 * BLOCKED on the scoped db — the wrapper cannot inject scope conditions into
 * relational queries, so exposing it would silently read across tenants.
 * Any access throws; use db.select() or an unsafe action's unsafeDb with an
 * explicit scope predicate instead.
 *
 * Escape hatch: declare the action `unsafe` and use `unsafeDb` (audited) with an
 * explicit scope predicate. Reaching a raw handle any other way is flagged at
 * compile time.
 *
 * Generated by Quickback Compiler.
 */
